---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout pageTitle="Privacy Policy">
  <section class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="legal-date">Last updated: August 2026</p>

    <h2>1. Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The Virtue Initiative is a non-profit pornography accountability application. This policy
      describes what data we collect, how we use it, how it is protected, and your rights regarding
      that data. We do not sell, advertise with, or monetize your personal data.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Data we collect</h2>
    <p>We collect the data needed to provide the service:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Account information</strong> - your email address and an optional display name, provided
        at sign-up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Encrypted screenshot data</strong> - screenshots and other encrypted monitoring batches
        captured by The Virtue Initiative clients installed on your devices. These batches may include
        screenshots and related encrypted event data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Device metadata</strong> - the name, platform, and activity timestamps of each device
        you register.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Batch metadata</strong> - unencrypted information sent alongside each encrypted batch
        so the service can index and summarize it without reading it. Your device sends the time range
        the batch covers and counts of the events and screenshots inside it; we store the device it came
        from, the time range, and how many of its events were scored as medium or high risk, along with
        the key material and integrity hash for that batch. We do not store the contents of any event.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Alert details</strong> - when a client records a high-risk event (for example, you stop
        monitoring, or a device stops reporting), it sends a short unencrypted summary of that event alongside
        the batch - its type, timestamp, risk score, and a brief title and description. We use that summary
        only to compose an alert email to your accountability partners, and we do not store it; the event
        itself is retained only in encrypted form. If you have no accepted partners, or they have turned
        alert emails off, no alert is sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Partner relationships</strong> - the email addresses of accountability partners you invite,
        and the permissions you grant them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Encryption and sharing records</strong> - encrypted key material and related records needed
        to restore your encrypted data and share access with authorized accountability partners.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Authentication and session data</strong> - access tokens, refresh tokens, registered device
        tokens, and similar session data needed to keep you signed in and connect devices to your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bug reports</strong> - if you choose to report a bug, we receive the description you write,
        an optional contact email address, and the platform, app version, and operating system details
        of the device you reported from. From a client app you may also choose to attach a recent excerpt
        of that device's operational log; the app tells you before it does so. These logs describe how
        the software behaved - they do not contain your screenshots. Bug reports are delivered to us by
        email and are not linked to your monitoring data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Who a bug report identifies</strong> - a report you send while signed in always tells
        us which account it came from, and a report sent from a client app signed in to your account always
        identifies that device by name and platform along with the account that owns it. Your account
        email address is included and is used as the address we reply to, whether or not you fill in the
        contact field. There is no way to send an identified report anonymously; if you do not want a
        report tied to your account, sign out first, or send it from our public site without signing in,
        in which case it carries only what you type into it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Donation records</strong> - if you donate, you are redirected to Stripe's own hosted checkout
        page to enter your payment details. Those details go straight to Stripe and are never sent to
        our servers, so we never see your full card number - Stripe shows us only the card brand, its
        expiry, and its last four digits. Stripe then reports the completed donation back to us, and we
        keep that record: your name, email address, phone number, the amount, whether the donation recurs,
        and Stripe's identifiers for you and for any recurring donation. Stripe may also collect a billing
        address; we do not store it in our own systems, but like the partial card details it is visible
        to us in our Stripe account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email preferences</strong> - how often you want to receive alert and summary emails, and
        whether your address has bounced or been reported as spam.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local device/browser storage</strong> - clients may store local credentials, encryption
        keys, or configuration values on your device or in your browser so the service can continue operating
        after restart. The web app sets one strictly necessary session cookie and stores your key material
        in your browser. We do not use analytics, advertising, or tracking cookies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Request metadata</strong> - like any web service, our servers process the IP address and
        user agent of incoming requests. We use these to apply rate limits and to protect the service,
        and we do not use them to build a profile of you.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. How we use your data</h2>
    <p>We use your data to provide and secure The Virtue Initiative service:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Clients may capture screenshots, monitor device or system state, and send encrypted batches
        and the metadata described above to our servers as part of the accountability service.
      </li>
      <li>
        Encrypted screenshot data is stored and later delivered so you and the accountability
        partners you explicitly authorize can view it with the appropriate decryption keys.
      </li>
      <li>
        Batch metadata is used to show activity and operational status to authorized users and
        partners, and to summarize activity in email digests, without decrypting the underlying
        events.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alert details are used to email your accountability partners when a high-risk event occurs,
        so that a partner learns about it even if they do not open the app. The alert names you, the
        device involved, and what happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        Stored batch metadata is used to build the periodic summary emails your partners have chosen
        to receive. Those summaries report counts and an estimate of how many screenshots are
        available, derived from how long each device was reporting - not from reading your data.
      </li>
      <li>
        Bug reports are used to diagnose and fix problems, and to reply to you if you gave us a way
        to reach you.
      </li>
      <li>
        Account, device, partner, and session data is used to authenticate you, operate registered
        clients, and manage sharing permissions.
      </li>
      <li>
        We do not use your screenshots, events, or bug reports for advertising, profiling, or model
        training.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Processing that happens on your device</h2>
    <p>Some analysis runs entirely on your own device, before anything is uploaded:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Text redaction</strong> - where the platform supports it, clients detect text in a screenshot
        and black it out before the screenshot is encrypted and uploaded, so passwords, messages, and
        other on-screen text are less likely to leave your device. This is a best-effort protection, not
        a guarantee; if text detection is unavailable or fails, the screenshot is uploaded without redaction.
        Detected text itself is never uploaded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Risk scoring</strong> - clients run a local image classifier to estimate whether a screenshot
        contains explicit content, and record that score with the event. The classifier runs on your device,
        is shipped with the app, and does not send your screenshots anywhere for analysis. Only the resulting
        scores and counts, not the screenshot, are visible to us.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Screenshot processing</strong> - screenshots are downscaled and blurred, then encrypted
        on your device before upload.
      </li>
      <li>We do not train models on your screenshots, logs, or any other data you send us.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On Android, the app uses an accessibility service solely to capture screenshots. It does not
      read, record, or transmit the content of the screen through that service, and it does not
      process accessibility events.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Data retention</h2>
    <p>
      Encrypted monitoring batches and their metadata are deleted automatically 30 days after they
      are created, both from our database and from object storage. Expired sessions and email tokens
      are pruned on the same schedule. This retention window is not currently configurable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Account, device, and partner records are retained for as long as your account exists. Deleting
      your account removes those records, along with your devices, sessions, and batch metadata,
      immediately; the encrypted batch blobs themselves are removed by the normal 30-day cycle.
      Donation records are retained as long as we need them for accounting and tax purposes. Bug
      reports live in our support mailbox and are retained as long as they are useful for diagnosis.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can delete your account yourself from the web app's settings. You may also request
      deletion by contacting us at
      <a href="mailto:help@virtueinitiative.org">help@virtueinitiative.org</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Data sharing</h2>
    <p>
      We do not sell your personal data. We share your data only as needed to operate the service,
      including with accountability partners you explicitly authorize and with the infrastructure
      providers listed below. Each provider's own privacy policy governs its handling of the data it
      processes for us.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> - hosts the API and web apps and stores account data and encrypted
        batches (Workers, D1, and R2 object storage), and provides the network tunnel to our tamper-evidence
        server.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Amazon Web Services</strong> - delivers our outgoing email (Simple Email Service) and
        reports bounces and complaints back to us. Email we send - alerts, digests, invitations, and account
        mail - passes through AWS, as do bug reports you submit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stripe</strong> - processes donations. Payment card details go directly to Stripe and
        are never handled by our servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our own tamper-evidence server</strong> - a server we operate that stores a running hash
        chain per device, used to detect whether monitoring data has been altered or removed. It stores
        hashes and device identifiers only, never your content.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We may also disclose data where required by law.</p>

    <h2>7. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Data is transmitted over HTTPS. Monitoring batches are encrypted on your device before upload,
      and the key for each batch is wrapped separately for you and for each partner you have
      authorized, so only those recipients can decrypt it. Those encrypted blobs may be retrievable
      from storage if their URLs are known, but they are intended to be unreadable without the
      relevant encryption keys. Batch metadata, alert details, account records, partner records, and
      other service metadata are stored server-side unencrypted and are intended to be accessible
      only through authorized account access. Passwords are hashed and are not stored in plaintext.
      Before a client attaches a log excerpt to a bug report, it scrubs anything that looks like a
      session token from the text.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Children</h2>
    <p>
      The Virtue Initiative is not intended for use by individuals under the age of 13. We do not
      knowingly collect personal data from children under 13.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You may request access to, correction of, or deletion of your personal data at any time by
      contacting us at
      <a href="mailto:help@virtueinitiative.org">help@virtueinitiative.org</a>. We will make
      reasonable efforts to respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We may update this policy from time to time. Material changes will be communicated by updating
      the "Last updated" date above. Continued use of the service after changes constitutes
      acceptance of the revised policy.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      For privacy-related questions or requests, contact us at
      <a href="mailto:help@virtueinitiative.org">help@virtueinitiative.org</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
